## metric

:::box{class=lead}
One figure and its caption. The label is the caption and the body is the figure — the reverse of
what most people write first, and the reason every number on the [home page](/#cost) was upside
down for a day.
:::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
:::metric[requests per second]
18,400
:::
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

:::metric[requests per second]
18,400
:::
:::

::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #badge}

:::box{class=eyebrow}
Text · label optional
:::

## badge

:::box{class=lead}
The only inline built-in. One colon, not three, because it belongs inside a sentence rather than
around a block.
:::

| Attribute | Type | Default | Accepts |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `kind` | enum | `info` | `info`, `warning`, `danger`, `success` |

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
A release is :badge[stable]{kind=success} once green,
:badge[rc]{kind=warning} while it is watched,
:badge[yanked]{kind=danger} if it must not be used,
and :badge[alpha] until any of that applies.
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

A release is :badge[stable]{kind=success} once green,
:badge[rc]{kind=warning} while it is watched,
:badge[yanked]{kind=danger} if it must not be used,
and :badge[alpha] until any of that applies.
:::

::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #box}

:::box{class=eyebrow}
Container · label forbidden
:::

## box

:::box{class=lead}
A wrapper that costs nothing: no default class, no stylesheet, only the `class` and `id` you
gave it. `as` swaps the element for a landmark, which is the one thing Markdown cannot say and a
real page needs — this document's header, sections and footer are all `box`.
:::

| Attribute | Type | Default | Accepts |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `as` | enum | `div` | `div`, `section`, `article`, `nav`, `header`, `footer`, `aside`, `main` |

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
:::box{as=aside class=sidenote}
Everything on this page is inside one of these.
:::
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

:::box{as=aside class=sidenote}
Everything on this page is inside one of these.
:::
:::

::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #controls}

:::box{class=eyebrow}
Container and leaf · label forbidden
:::

## button, input and form

:::box{class=lead}
These three exist so that events have native elements to attach to. They are ordinary
`<button>`, `<input>` and `<form>` elements — no wrapper, no widget — and they are the reason
this page is in `app` trust. The counter below is live.
:::

| Component | Attribute | Type | Default | Accepts |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `button` | `type` | enum | `button` | `button`, `submit`, `reset` |
| `input` | `type` | enum | `text` | `text` |
| `input` | `value` | string | empty | any string |
| `input` | `name` | string | — | any string |
| `form` | — | — | — | — |

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
::state{clicks=0 who=Ada}

:::form
::input{name=visitor value=Ada on-input="who = who"}

:::button{on-click="clicks = clicks + 1"}
Count
:::
:::

{{ who }} clicked {{ clicks }} times.
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

:::form
::input{name=visitor value=Ada on-input="who = who"}

:::button{on-click="clicks = clicks + 1"}
Count
:::
:::

{{ who }} clicked {{ clicks }} times.
:::

::::

:::box{class=lead}
Writing the same name on both sides of `on-input` is how a text input binds: the runtime coerces
what was typed to that state's declared type before the handler runs. The handler itself is not
JavaScript. It can assign, compare and do arithmetic, and it cannot reach the
network, storage, timers or any host object — which is why compiling a document someone else
wrote is safe, and why the copy buttons in the source panes above are host code rather than
something this document was able to ask for.
:::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #more}

:::box{class=eyebrow}
Beyond the built-ins
:::

## Your own

:::box{class=lead}
A `.hmx` file in `components/` is a component: its frontmatter declares the attributes it takes,
its body is the markup, and a `<style scoped>` block styles only what it emitted. The nine above
are simply the ones that ship, and they are registered through the same interface.
:::

